Supplementary Material for
Proceeding on the riddles of ketene pyrolysis by applying ab initio quantum
chemical computational methods in a detailed kinetic modeling study
Heiko Minwegen, Malte Döntgen, Yann Fenard, Philipp Morsch, Karl Alexander Heufer
Proc. Combust. Inst. 38, 2021

This document contains the primary results from the quantum chemical computations
on structures, frequencies, single point energies and potentials of the
potential energy surfaces stationary points used for the thermochemistry and
reaction rate constants calculations.

Additionally, separated input files of the reaction networks and decomposition
reactions computed with the Master Equation System Solver (MESS) can be found 
at the end of this file.

###CONTENT

##PART1
Geometries, Frequencies, rotational potentials, electronic single point energies (SPE)
of stationary points

'Species denotation in (ketene.inp,ketene.dat,ketene.trans)' --> 'SMILES'
Basis set definitions: 
DZ   -> cc-pVDZ
TZ   -> cc-pVTZ
CBS  -> Extrapolation to the basis set limit with DZ and TZ 

#C0                     #C1                    #C2
H      --> [H]          CO     --> [C]=O       C2H    --> [C]#C
H2     --> [H][H]       CO2    --> O=C=O       C2O    --> [C]=C=O
O      --> [O]          CH     --> [CH]        C2H2   --> C#C
OH     --> [OH]         CH2    --> [CH2]       C2H3   --> [CH]=C
HO2    --> [O]O         CH2(S) --> [CH2]       C2H4   --> C=C
H2O2   --> OO           CH3    --> [CH3]       C2H5   --> C[CH2]
                        CH4    --> C           H2CC   --> [C]=C
                                               HCCO   --> [CH]=C=O
                                               HCCOH  --> C#CO
                                               CH2CO  --> C=C=O
                                               CH3CO  --> C[C]=O
                                               HCOHCO --> O=C-C=O

#C3                                            #C4
C3H3    --> [CH]=C=C                           DHCCO   --> O=C=CC=C=O
C2H3CO  --> [CH2]C=C=O                         DCH2CO  --> C=C1OC(=O)C1
CYC3H2O --> C1=CC1(=O)                         DHCCOyl --> O=C=[C]C=C=O
C3H4-A  --> C=C=C                              
C3H4-P  --> C#CC                               Intermediates C2H2+HCCO<=>C3H3+CO
CC3H4   --> C1=CC1                             [CH]=CC=C=O (C3H3CO, Int1)    
CYC3HO  --> C1=[C]C1=O                         C=[C]C=C=O (C3H3CO-2, Int2)  

Intermediate CYC3H2O(+M)<=>C2H2+CO(+M)
[CH]=C[C]=O  

Intermediate C3H4-A(+M)<=>C2H2+CH2(+M)
[CH2]C=[CH]

#TS Transition states
  CH2CO+H<=>HCCO+H2
  CH2CO+CH2<=>HCCO+CH3
  CH2CO+CH3<=>HCCO+CH4
  CH2CO+HO2<=>HCCO+H2O2
  C2H2+HCCO<=>C3H3+CO TS1
  C2H2+HCCO<=>C3H3+CO TS2
  C2H2+HCCO<=>C3H3+CO TS3
  CYC3H2O(+M)<=>C2H2+CO(+M) TS1
  CYC3H2O(+M)<=>C2H2+CO(+M) TS2
  CYC3H2O+CH3<=>C2H2+CH3CO
  CH2CO(+M)<=>HCCOH(+M)
  DCH2CO(+M)<=>CH2CO+CH2CO(+M)
  DCH2CO(+M)<=>C3H4-A+CO2(+M)
  DHCCO(+M)<=>CO+CYC3H2O(+M)
  C2H4+H<=>C2H3+H2
  C2H4+CH2<=>C2H3+CH3
  C2H2+H<=>C2H+H2
  C2H2+CH2<=>C2H+CH3
  C2H2+CH3<=>C2H+CH4
  C2H3CO(+M)<=>CO+C2H3(+M)
  C3H4-A(+M)<=>C2H2+CH2(+M) TS1
  C3H4-A(+M)<=>C2H2+CH2(+M) TS2
  
